  "final": "Both the public checks and my extended edge-case suite pass. Here's a summary of the change:\n\n## Changes\n\n**`src/repository.py`**\n- `find(connection, tenant, owner, document_id)` — the query now filters on all three conditions: `id = ? AND tenant = ? AND owner = ? AND deleted_at IS NULL`. Any unavailable ID (missing, cross-tenant, cross-owner, or soft-deleted) yields the same `None` result with no way to distinguish the cause.\n- `listing(connection, tenant, owner)` — scoped by `tenant = ? AND owner = ?` with `deleted_at IS NULL`, keeping the existing `ORDER BY id`. Removed the old owner-only scope (which previously leaked foreign tenants' rows and deleted documents).\n- Both queries use bound `?` parameters exclusively, so names containing quotes (e.g. `t'x`, `o\"y`) are handled safely with no string interpolation.\n- `decode` still returns exactly `{\"id\", \"title\", \"body\"}` — no deletion metadata is exposed. `initialize` is unchanged.\n\n**`src/service.py`**\n- `get_document` and `list_documents` now forward their `tenant` and `owner` context (previously `get_document` ignored both entirely, and `list_documents` ignored `tenant`), which was the source of the cross-tenant/cross-owner and deleted-record leaks.\n\n## Verification\n- `python3 checks/public.py` → passes.\n- Extended suite confirmed: own-scope reads work while deleted (2), cross-tenant (3), cross-owner (4), and nonexistent (99) IDs all return `None`; quoted tenant/owner names are stored and matched correctly; other scopes only reach their own rows; listings are ordered by id (`[0, 1, 10]`), exclude soft-deleted rows, and contain only the `id`/`title`/`body` fields; `None` scopes match nothing.",
